      "latency_ms": 2376.9,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 620,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on the surface of Earth. It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, explained through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\n* **Chemical Formula:** $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$\n* **Composition:** Each molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **States of Matter:** Water is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n  * **Liquid** (the water we drink, rivers, oceans)\n  * **Solid** (ice, snow, glaciers)\n  * **Gas** (water vapor, steam, humidity in the air)\n\n### 2. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater has several unique and unusual properties that make it vital for life:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is how our bodies transport nutrients and how oceans maintain their chemical balance.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of tall trees).\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat without drastically changing its own temperature. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps living organisms from overheating.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. This is why ice floats on water. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n\n### 3. The Ecological and Geological View\n* **Earth's Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **The Water Cycle (Hydrologic Cycle):** Water is constantly recycled on Earth. It evaporates from the oceans and land, rises into the atmosphere to form clouds, falls back down as rain or snow (precipitation), and flows back into the oceans via rivers. \n\n### 4. The Biological View\n* **The Basis of Life:** Water provides the medium in which the metabolic and cellular processes of life take place. \n* **Human Body:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. It regulates body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and protects organs and tissues. Humans can survive for weeks without food, but only a few days without water.\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties that make it the very foundation of weather, geology, and life itself."
